<rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"><channel><title>NYT &gt; Technology</title><link>https://www.nytimes.com/section/technology</link><description>Technology industry news, commentary, and analysis, with reporting on big tech, startups, and internet culture. The New York Times is a hub for conversation about news and ideas.</description><atom:link rel="self" href="https://rss.nytimes.com/services/xml/rss/nyt/Technology.xml" type="application/rss+xml" /><item><title>We All Do Errands. Noah Blau Films Them.</title><link>https://www.nytimes.com/2026/07/31/style/errands-should-be-the-most-boring-show-on-the-internet.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.nytimes.com/2026/07/31/style/errands-should-be-the-most-boring-show-on-the-internet.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</guid><atom:link rel="standout" href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/07/31/style/errands-should-be-the-most-boring-show-on-the-internet.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss" /><description>Fighting a traffic ticket. Hanging a curtain. Installing an air-conditioner. With his cult-hit online series, Noah Blau has made the mundane fascinating.</description><dc:creator>Alex Vadukul</dc:creator><pubDate>Fri, 31 Jul 2026 09:04:17 GMT</pubDate><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Social Media</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Video Recordings</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Downloads and Streaming</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Errands</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Noah Blau</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Bushwick Brooklyn</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">NYC</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_geo">New York City</category><media:content url="https://static01.nyt.com/images/2026/08/02/fashion/30ST-ERRANDS-01-wgcj/30ST-ERRANDS-01-wgcj-videoSixteenByNineJumbo1600.jpg" medium="image"><media:credit>Lanna Apisukh for The New York Times</media:credit><media:description>Noah Blau, the creator of the online video series “Errands,” and Aitana Doyle, an actress, on the streets of Bushwick in Brooklyn, during the making of a recent episode.</media:description></media:content></item><item><title>Man’s Claim That Brakes Failed on Jaguar Was Scam to Avoid Car Payments, Court Rules</title><link>https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/04/world/europe/runaway-car-jaguar-uk.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/04/world/europe/runaway-car-jaguar-uk.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</guid><atom:link rel="standout" href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/04/world/europe/runaway-car-jaguar-uk.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss" /><description>Nathan Owen called the police, saying he was trapped in a runaway car. A judge determined it was a lie and sentenced him to more than four years in prison.</description><dc:creator>Victor Mather</dc:creator><pubDate>Tue, 04 Aug 2026 17:08:07 GMT</pubDate><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Cars</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Car Crash</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Jaguar Land Rover</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Fraud</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">England</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Police</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Driving Under the Influence DUI</category><media:content url="https://static01.nyt.com/images/2026/08/04/multimedia/04xp-runaway-car-top-grab-02/04xp-runaway-car-top-grab-02-videoSixteenByNineJumbo1600.jpg" medium="image"><media:credit>Merseyside Police</media:credit><media:description>An image from a video released by the Merseyside Police showing that Nathan Owen’s Jaguar had functioning brake lights when he claimed his brakes were not working. </media:description></media:content></item><item><title>A SpaceX Rocket Will Soon Crash Into the Moon</title><link>https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/04/science/spacex-rocket-moon-crash.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/04/science/spacex-rocket-moon-crash.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</guid><atom:link rel="standout" href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/04/science/spacex-rocket-moon-crash.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss" /><description>The second stage of a Falcon 9 rocket, which sent two private landers to the moon last year, will collide with the moon early Wednesday morning.</description><dc:creator>Kenneth Chang</dc:creator><pubDate>Tue, 04 Aug 2026 09:04:37 GMT</pubDate><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Space</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Rocket</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Moon</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Private spaceflight</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Planet</category><media:content url="https://static01.nyt.com/images/2026/08/04/multimedia/04SCI-SpaceX-Moon-Crash-ROCKET-klqw/04SCI-SpaceX-Moon-Crash-ROCKET-klqw-videoSixteenByNineJumbo1600.jpg" medium="image"><media:credit>Gregg Newton/Agence France-Presse</media:credit><media:description>A SpaceX Falcon 9 rocket carrying two private lunar landers launched in January last year. The second stage of this rocket will hit the moon at 5,400 miles per hour on Wednesday.</media:description></media:content></item><item><title>In Lawsuit, NJ Accuses Amazon of Suppressing Pay for Delivery Drivers</title><link>https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/04/technology/amazon-nj-lawsuit.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/04/technology/amazon-nj-lawsuit.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</guid><atom:link rel="standout" href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/04/technology/amazon-nj-lawsuit.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss" /><description>The state attorney general said in an antitrust lawsuit filed on Tuesday that the company was abusing its market power to keep its delivery costs low.</description><dc:creator>Karen Weise</dc:creator><pubDate>Tue, 04 Aug 2026 18:32:54 GMT</pubDate><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">New Jersey</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Amazon</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">E Commerce</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Lawsuits</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Delivery Services</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Competition law</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Jennifer Davenport</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Wages and salaries</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Jobs</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Job Recruiting and Hiring</category><media:content url="https://static01.nyt.com/images/2026/08/04/multimedia/04biz-amazon-nj-htwk/04biz-amazon-nj-htwk-videoSixteenByNineJumbo1600.jpg" medium="image"><media:credit>Vincent Alban/The New York Times</media:credit><media:description>Amazon said the lawsuit wrongly characterized the company’s activities, and that its contractor delivery partners were free to operate as they saw fit.</media:description></media:content></item><item><title>Five Takeaways From the Times Investigation Into Larry Ellison’s A.I. Gamble</title><link>https://www.nytimes.com/2026/07/31/magazine/takeaways-larry-ellison-oracle-ai.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.nytimes.com/2026/07/31/magazine/takeaways-larry-ellison-oracle-ai.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</guid><atom:link rel="standout" href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/07/31/magazine/takeaways-larry-ellison-oracle-ai.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss" /><description>The billionaire’s company took on ominous loads of debt to build data centers around the world. The implications go far beyond Oracle.</description><dc:creator>Jonathan Mahler, Jim Rutenberg and Kirsten Grind</dc:creator><pubDate>Fri, 31 Jul 2026 09:05:04 GMT</pubDate><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Lawrence J Ellison</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Oracle</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Artificial intelligence</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Data center</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Computers and the Internet</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Tech Industry</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">OpenAI</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">TikTok</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Bytedance</category><media:content url="https://static01.nyt.com/images/2026/08/16/magazine/16mag-Larry-Takeaways/16mag-Larry-Takeaways-videoSixteenByNineJumbo1600.jpg" medium="image"><media:credit>Andrew Harnik/Getty Images</media:credit><media:description>Larry Ellison spoke at the first news conference of the second Trump administration, where the president called him the “C.E.O. of everything.”</media:description></media:content></item><item><title>Why TikTok’s Algorithm Keeps You Trapped in a Breakup Loop</title><link>https://www.nytimes.com/2026/07/26/technology/personaltech/tiktok-instagram-breakup-content.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.nytimes.com/2026/07/26/technology/personaltech/tiktok-instagram-breakup-content.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</guid><atom:link rel="standout" href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/07/26/technology/personaltech/tiktok-instagram-breakup-content.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss" /><description>As social media companies face scrutiny over addictive design features, mental health experts cite a stream of breakup content as an example of the failure to protect users.</description><dc:creator>Jonathan Wolfe</dc:creator><pubDate>Sun, 26 Jul 2026 09:00:27 GMT</pubDate><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Social Media</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">TikTok</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Mental Health</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Dating</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">internal-open-access-from-nl</category><media:content url="https://static01.nyt.com/images/2026/07/20/arts/00xp-tiktok-breakup-cover/00xp-tiktok-breakup-cover-videoSixteenByNineJumbo1600.jpg" medium="image" /></item><item><title>How to Use ChatGPT and Gemini Prompts to Find Out What They Know About You</title><link>https://www.nytimes.com/2026/07/23/technology/personaltech/chatgpt-gemini-prompts-privacy.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.nytimes.com/2026/07/23/technology/personaltech/chatgpt-gemini-prompts-privacy.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</guid><a