reator>Brian X. Chen</dc:creator><pubDate>Thu, 30 Jul 2026 09:03:08 GMT</pubDate><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Apple</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Artificial intelligence</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Software</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">None</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">iPhone</category><media:content url="https://static01.nyt.com/images/2026/07/28/business/00techfix/00techfix-videoSixteenByNineJumbo1600.jpg" medium="image"><media:credit>Sisi Yu</media:credit></media:content></item><item><title>A U.S. Citizen Deleted His Phone’s Data. Now He Faces a Felony Charge.</title><link>https://www.nytimes.com/2026/07/28/us/duress-password-phone-wipe-charge.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.nytimes.com/2026/07/28/us/duress-password-phone-wipe-charge.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</guid><atom:link rel="standout" href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/07/28/us/duress-password-phone-wipe-charge.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss" /><description>Federal prosecutors charged a man returning to the United States with obstruction because he gave them a passcode that erased his smartphone during a customs search.</description><dc:creator>Adeel Hassan</dc:creator><pubDate>Tue, 28 Jul 2026 21:54:49 GMT</pubDate><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Search and seizure</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Smartphone</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Android</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Customs and Border Protection US</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Software</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Airport</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Airport security</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Privacy</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Electronic Communications Privacy Act</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Atlanta</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">US</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">internal-open-access-from-nl</category><media:content url="https://static01.nyt.com/images/2026/07/27/multimedia/27xp-phone-wipe-wkvp/27xp-phone-wipe-wkvp-videoSixteenByNineJumbo1600.jpg" medium="image"><media:credit>Annegret Hilse/Reuters</media:credit><media:description>A U.S. citizen used an open-source operating system, GrapheneOS, on his Google Pixel phone to “delete the digital contents,” before the device was seized, an indictment against the man said.</media:description></media:content></item><item><title>SpaceX’s Stock Lockup Is Expiring. Prepare for a Bumpy Ride.</title><link>https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/03/technology/spacex-lockup-stock-market.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/03/technology/spacex-lockup-stock-market.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</guid><atom:link rel="standout" href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/03/technology/spacex-lockup-stock-market.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss" /><description>On Thursday, employees and other insiders at Elon Musk’s rocket company will be released from their first stock “lockup” and can start selling some of their shares.</description><dc:creator>Ryan Mac</dc:creator><pubDate>Mon, 03 Aug 2026 14:22:59 GMT</pubDate><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">SpaceX</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Elon Musk</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">IPO</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Stocks</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Bonds</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Private spaceflight</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Earnings Reports</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Short Selling</category><media:content url="https://static01.nyt.com/images/2026/08/04/business/00spacex-lockup/00spacex-lockup-videoSixteenByNineJumbo1600.jpg" medium="image"><media:credit>Michael Parkin</media:credit></media:content></item><item><title>White House Whipsaws Silicon Valley (and Itself) Over A.I. Rules</title><link>https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/04/technology/ai-washington-regulation-whiplash.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/04/technology/ai-washington-regulation-whiplash.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</guid><atom:link rel="standout" href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/04/technology/ai-washington-regulation-whiplash.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss" /><description>The Trump administration has struggled with how to approach “open source” models, which are freely available to download and favored by Chinese companies.</description><dc:creator>David McCabe, Mike Isaac, Ana Swanson and Kate Conger</dc:creator><pubDate>Tue, 04 Aug 2026 09:04:23 GMT</pubDate><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Artificial intelligence</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Computers and the Internet</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Tech Industry</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">US Politics</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Regulation and Deregulation</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Executive Orders</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Politics</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Lobbying</category><media:content url="https://static01.nyt.com/images/2026/08/03/multimedia/03biz-ai-whiplash--03-chmf/03biz-ai-whiplash--03-chmf-videoSixteenByNineJumbo1600.jpg" medium="image"><media:credit>Agence France-Presse — Getty Images</media:credit><media:description>Chinese open-source artificial models like Kimi, made by Moonshot AI, have added a new and urgent dimension to the fight between Washington and Beijing over who will control critical technologies.</media:description></media:content></item><item><title>Trump White House Readies AI Framework to Review Security Risks</title><link>https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/04/technology/white-house-ai-framework.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/04/technology/white-house-ai-framework.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</guid><atom:link rel="standout" href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/04/technology/white-house-ai-framework.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss" /><description>The voluntary review process will cover closed-source artificial intelligence models, but exclude those that publish the underlying code.</description><dc:creator>David McCabe, Mike Isaac, Kate Conger and Ana Swanson</dc:creator><pubDate>Tue, 04 Aug 2026 22:17:11 GMT</pubDate><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Artificial intelligence</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Computers and the Internet</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Tech Industry</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Regulation and Deregulation</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Executive Orders</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Computer security</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">US Politics</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Open-Source Software</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Donald Trump</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Anthropic</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Google</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">OpenAI</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Meta</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">China</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">US</category><media:content url="https://static01.nyt.com/images/2026/08/04/multimedia/04biz-ai-trump-vbcg/04biz-ai-trump-vbcg-videoSixteenByNineJumbo1600.jpg" medium="image"><media:credit>Haiyun Jiang/The New York Times</media:credit><media:description>President Trump at the G7 summit with Sam Altman of OpenAI in June. The Trump administration laid out how the federal government plans to review whether new A.I. tools pose a security risk.</media:description></media:content></item><item><title>SpaceX, in First Earnings After IPO, Reports Soaring AI Spending</title><link>https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/04/technology/spacex-earnings-elon-musk.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/04/technology/spacex-earnings-elon-musk.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</guid><atom:link rel="standout" href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/04/technology/spacex-earnings-elon-musk.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss" /><description>Elon Musk’s rocket company said